If we can specify what kinds of problems it applies to, I
see no problem introducing something that's limited.  We can
add different variants as we go.

I'd think doubly exponential [i.e., exp(-exp(abs(x)))]
would be very limiting, but I have no clue as to what kinds
of applications people would use this for.

Cook points to more of an explanation here:

  http://www.johndcook.com/blog/double_exponential_integration/

- Bob

