MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Ben.  Oh, I didn’t realize you’d done all this.  I did some more simulations and it looks like the distribution of these coefs may approach some fixed dist as K approaches infinity.  See attached figure, where I did LKJ(1), Wishart(K+1) and inv-Wishart(K+1) (in the latter case normalizing so that they gave corr matrices rather than cov matrices) and then I looked at the histogram of all the regression coefs.  For the LKJ, at least, it looks like the dist of the betas might be approaching a fixed limit as K increases.

Those histograms are hard to read, so also here are some quantiles of the betas for LKJ(1) for each of several K’s:

    K = 5, 1/5/25/50/75/95/99 percentiles are -2.1, -1.1, -0.5,  0.0,  0.5,  1.2,  2.1
  K = 10, 1/5/25/50/75/95/99 percentiles are -2.3, -1.2, -0.4,  0.0,  0.4,  1.2,  2.2
  K = 20, 1/5/25/50/75/95/99 percentiles are -2.5, -1.3, -0.5,  0.0,  0.5,  1.3,  2.5
  K = 50, 1/5/25/50/75/95/99 percentiles are -2.5, -1.3, -0.4,  0.0,  0.4,  1.3,  2.5
K = 100, 1/5/25/50/75/95/99 percentiles are -2.6, -1.3, -0.4,  0.0,  0.4,  1.3,  2.6

Strikingly stable, I’d say.  And reasonably fit by a t distribution with 2.5 df and scale .5:

> pfround(quantile(.5*rt(100000, 2.5), c(.01, .05, .25, .5, .75, .95, .99)), 1)
  1%   5%  25%  50%  75%  95%  99%
-2.6 -1.3 -0.4  0.0  0.4  1.3  2.6

See you
A

