This was troubling me, so I re-ran it yesterday having shut down every other process (running under Windows, ho hum) I could. But I also think the Stata timings themselves look weird, as in they print a cumulative time (which makes sense) and an incremental time (which doesn't). This time I've taken the cumulative time and the strange pattern is gone. Lesson: use built-in timers by all means, but print the machine date/time to a log file at the same time as a sanity check.

On Monday, December 21, 2015 at 12:29:13 AM UTC, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> I suspect the timing issues were occurring because in certain runs, Stan’s compile time was being included in the calculations?
> A
>
> > On Dec 20, 2015, at 6:14 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> >
> > In working on a paper related to StataStan, Robert Grant
> > (author of StataStan) was finding odd timings, so I thought
> > I'd run a simple comparison.  I used the following model:
> >
> >  parameters {
> >    matrix[20,20] alpha[10];
> >  }
> >  model {
> >    for (i in 1:10)
> >      to_vector(alpha[i]) ~ normal(0, 1);
> >  }
> >
> > One chain of RStan and CmdStan run in almost exactly the
> > same time for the same seed.  In this case, RStan's doing
> > even more work in that it's saving the warmup iterations,
> > whereas CmdStan isn't.
> >
> > So it sure doesn't look like RStan is adding any overhead here.
> >
> > - Bob
